[COMMENTS]
STATION,Velocity area station on the River Teviot in the east Southern Uplands of Scotland. GENERAL: Velocity area station; about 30m wide natural section. STATION HYDROMETRY: The primary control is a gravel shoal (~100m) below the station that is prone to adjustment during floods. A ford 200m downstream and a mill weir 650m downstream may be an influence on the stage discharge control at higher flows. Bypassing can occur on left bank at levels above about 2m, although this is thought to account for less the 10% of flows during extreme events. Frequent re-rating due to weed growth, but only two ratings are required across the period of record for peak flows. Gauged up to QMED with simple extrapolation beyond. FLOW RECORD: October 2005 event was exceptional. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in September 2019 (WINFAP Files v8) and September 2022 (WINFAP Files v11). FLOW REGIME: Natural regime.
CATCHMENT,Natural upland catchment. Bedrock mostly Silurian shale; predominantly overlain by superficial deposits. Hill grazing is the dominant land use but forestry is important in the headwaters (Craik Forest). Hawick is the only significant settlement. No significant catchment changes known.
Qmed Suitability,Gauged to QMED.
Pooling Suitability,Although only gauged to QMED, assessment of bypassing suggest it accounts for less than 10% of flows in higher events (above 2m).
[END]
